  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <b>AWS CodeCommit repositories encrypt all data both at rest and during transmission, so no additional actions are necessary for audit compliance.</b><br><br>By design, AWS CodeCommit automatically encrypts repository contents at rest using AWS-managed keys and secures all data in transit via HTTPS—no manual configuration for encryption is needed. This aligns with industry best practices and generally satisfies external audit requirements for encryption.<br><br><i>Developers should enable repository-level encryption when creating a new CodeCommit repository, which ensures encryption in transit and at rest as required by audits.</i> is incorrect because CodeCommit does not require activating encryption when creating a repo—encryption is enforced by default with no repository-level toggle.<br><br><i>A KMS encryption key must be provisioned specifically for CodeCommit, and then configured to enforce the use of this key for all data held or transmitted by CodeCommit.</i> is also wrong. Although AWS manages encryption with AWS-owned KMS keys for CodeCommit data, customers cannot directly assign a customer-managed KMS key to CodeCommit.<br><br><i>Although at rest encryption is enabled by default for CodeCommit, the team needs to manually configure encryption settings for data in transit, such as during 'git push' operations.</i> is not correct because all network operations with CodeCommit, including git commands, are already protected using industry-standard protocols such as HTTPS. No additional setup is necessary.<br><br><b>Tip:</b> For security audits, document AWS's built-in encryption and refer to the AWS CodeCommit security documentation. If stricter requirements exist (such as using only customer-managed keys), communicate any service limitations to your audit team.</p>",
  "query": "<p>Your company’s DevOps group is evaluating AWS CodeCommit as a replacement for GitHub. The organization has robust security requirements, and all tooling must meet stringent, third-party audit standards, especially regarding encryption of sensitive data.<br>What steps, if any, should the DevOps team take to guarantee that CodeCommit usage will be compliant with audit requirements for data encryption?</p>",
